after nearly nine years we're getting  our first playable taste of Final  Fantasy 15 with episode dusk a 15 looks  like the most promising final fantasy in  a while with other recent titles  dividing the fandom we finally remember  the series golden age spanning its birth  on the NES to Final Fantasy 10 on the  PlayStation to make no mistake we're  excited for 15 but we've been hurt  before so here are a few lessons we  think it can learn from the series  classic titles the overworld is key in a  good RPG classic Final Fantasy titles  took this to heart building sprawling  maps that made the world feel huge and  lived in many allowed you to traverse  them on foot searching for the forest  cave or city in which your next  objective SAT some areas head side  quests where you could glean some extra  Gil experience points and even new  characters but more recent titles  notably 13 are linear to the point of  being hallways guiding you from point A  to point B there is a thrill to running  around green fields as a Dane and final  fantasy nine or jumping on an airship to  another continent in 6 giving players  the freedom to explore and discover  things on their own added depth to the  experience from what we've seen a 15 it  looks like a semi linear experience we  do know you can walk or drive across the  entire map and the demo features the  party roaming freely in large areas what  we don't know is if areas will be  blocked off until the story progresses  their previous games bent these rules a  bit allowing you to familiarize yourself  with places that wouldn't become a big  deal until later one of the best  examples of this is how final fantasy  nine treats introducing playable  character quinny-quinn quina lives in  cuse marsh a tiny area accessible early  on but not important until halfway  through disc 1 if you go to the marsh  after wrapping up things in lindblom you  can recruit quina however you can also  choose to avoid the marsh until after  completing three more areas during which  time you can get Quinn up pretty much  whenever depending on when you recruit  quina the story changes and you could  recruit not one but two optional  characters in Final Fantasy 7 yuffie and  Vincent which also impacted the story  this kind of non-linearity gives you  more control in what is already a highly  scripted story it's immersive in ways  guiding characters down to singular path  is  not even if you jam that hallway with  pit stops for cutscenes it also makes  side quests and activities feel more  organic you can play blitzball anytime  you go to lucca in Final Fantasy 10 you  can breed chocobos at your leisure in  seven and don't forget triple triad and  tetra master the in-game collectible  card battling games in 8 and 9  populating the world with smaller  activities that don't involve saving it  makes things feel more real more  complete so what are the implications of  15 s one giant map does this mean  everything will be open from the start  similar to the first part of 9 or will  we be limited by the narrative final  fantasy is known for its colorful casts  protagonists antagonists and PCs you  watch them fight struggle and sometimes  die strong characters are vital because  if your character is weak sauce your  audience's interest in powering through  their adventure will be watery 2 15's  Noctis appears to be the primary focus  as far as characters go and if he's  going to be up front he should be the  most complex his most recent predecessor  lightning is a badass in her own right  but most of the time that's her only ski  remember clouds horrible PTSD after  watching his friend die and then he  lived as his friend mixing up his own  memories and leading a genuinely painful  existence to watch let alone play  through we need that a protag that's  relatable but not boring complicated but  not untouchable also key a good bad guy  we don't quite know who the big daddy is  in 15 but we're hoping he or she recalls  those who came before like kefka palazzo  the sassy mage and clown makeup who  destroyed the world with a smile and 6  he's the perfect balance of entertaining  and threatening chaotic you'll at its  finest when he was on screen it was all  about him but when he wasn't you still  felt his ominous presence looming over  all the triple threat of ject see more  and yu yevon in 10 did it as well tended  an excellent job of building up a  multi-tiered menace presenting danger  from all sides and taking the entire  game to unravel the emotional  complications that came with it 7 did  this to a disturbed guy with a sword  seeking out his own brand of justice and  on top of that the less than  well-intentioned Shinra corporation  rufus in the turks were interesting  complex another thing to worry about but  another set of characters you couldn't  wait to encounter  recent Final Fantasy games haven't had  the most interesting or relatable or  understandable villains Caius ballad  came close in final fantasy 13-2 with  his brooding vow to save his only friend  from an eternity of torment but  lightning returns watered down his  madness making his final confrontation  more cheesy than cathartic we're hoping  that 15 can learn from this setting up a  complex antagonistic presence and  pinning players under non-stop pressure  that brings us to a point that latter  day final fantasies have struggled with  we need narratives with meaningful  complexity final fantasy 12 was a  reskinned less interesting Star Wars the  13 trilogy could have been a solid story  but was thinned out across too many  games and these stories always start  with the opening cutscene the first ten  minutes of a game are crucial to  conveying tone in setting the stage in  terms of what players can expect many  Final Fantasy titles go right for the  cold open sea Final Fantasy 6 and the  march of the magitek armor sevens  bombing mission 8 slippery fatali you  remember those cutscenes there would  hooked you when sin crashed the Blitz  ball game those first notes of other  worlds stuck with you forever if you're  going to demand a 50-plus our investment  for one game you need a multi-level plot  with even pacing Final Fantasy 7 has  great narrative design you have cloud  who is part of an eco-terrorism group  trying to take down a mega-corporation  harvesting the world's energy to power  civilization which is also killing the  planet cloud used to work for said  corporation and while their lost one  friend to death and at mother to madness  his repressed memories bubble up through  the entire game and every character you  roll into your party has deep investment  in this story's outcome or something  riding on the narrative as a player we  require characters to be invested in  their own game otherwise why would we  and peppered throughout all of this of  course are powerful scenes moments of  dramatic tension segments that make you  tingly these are the high points pieces  that never leave you finding out sin is  your dad and then having to kill your  own summons garnet forgiving queen  brahne a as she lies dying on the beach  eros is death cell a suicide attempt the  Opera seen these moments work because  they're populated with strong characters  you have to give a character life before  you can throw them into a high-tension  situation or kill them off it's why it  was hard to care when Sarah turned into  a crystal in 13 she  was boring whatever but with the  franchise's almost 30 years of lessons  learned Final Fantasy 15 is poised to  stab us through the heart once more  Final Fantasy 15 already has us  trembling with optimism we hope Square  Enix has been listening to its fans and  reached back into the series history for  elements that worked well before there's  a lot of potential here for Final  Fantasy 15 so long as developers aren't  afraid to listen to its predecessors  [Music]
 

